WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:06.660
Hello there!

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.650
In the last video, we outlined the history
of the Transformer.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:19.790
I hope that now that you know about the Transformer’s
success and proliferation, you now are curious

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.320
enough to see how it works!

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:27.390
Because in this video, Ms. Coffee Bean will
help me briefly explain the Transformer architecture.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:32.369
We will explain the Transformers for sequence
to sequence modelling, that we employ in machine

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.270
translation for example.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:40.190
We remember that Recurrent Neural Networks,
the precursors of the transformers, use to

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:44.559
process the text sequentially, word AFTER
word.

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:49.470
They produce a vector from a whole sequence,
hoping to capture the meaning of the whole

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:50.470
sentence.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:55.360
This is of course problematic with long and
complicated sentences.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:02.180
Then from this vector, another RNN block is
decoding to the target sequence, in this case,

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.250
it is decoding to the translation of the sentence.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:12.070
So one part here in green, we can call from
now on the encoder, and the one in orange

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:13.800
red, is the decoder.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.780
But what is it that Transformers do differently?

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:23.720
The key with the transformers, is that while
RNNs process input piece AFTER piece, the

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.670
Transformer processes the whole sequence at
ONCE.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:31.310
And with parallelization comes a lot of the
power of the Transformer.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:36.000
Now we know what is done, but not how it is
done.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.880
It is now time to get into the details of
the Transformer architecture.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:46.220
Usually, the Transformer encoder is actually
a stack of encoders in layers.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.510
Stacking the encoders, enabled us to process
the sequence not only once, but a couple of

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:51.510
times.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.460
And what we do for the encoder, we also do
for the decoder.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.980
The idea behind stacking is to be able to
capture raw properties of the sentence first,

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.600
moving to more elaborate properties.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.820
To exemplify: It has been shown that these
layers of the Transformer architecture are

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:15.570
discovering the NLP pipeline: the first layers
focus on identifying part-of-speech tags,

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:19.940
followed by constituents, dependencies, semantic
roles and coreference.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:26.500
And of course, this structure is a little
fuzzier in reality, but it is there!

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:31.140
Now let us dissect one of these transformer
encoder layers.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:36.760
All encoder layers are built of the same kind
of blocks: The self-attention layer that processes

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:41.890
the input and transmits it to a “normal”
feed forward neural network.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.950
We will explain self-attention shortly.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:49.560
After we process the input through a couple
of these encoders, we are ready to construct

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:54.590
a sequence, which should be in this example
the translation of the input after we pass

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.780
it through the decoder.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:01.580
For the decoder, we have the same two blocks
as for the encoder with a special encoder-decoder

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.500
attention layer in the middle.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:09.290
So far, we listed the order of the layers
that form the transformer blocks.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.940
Now for explaining what these layers do exactly,
it is vector/tensor-flow time!

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:19.770
We say vectors, because of course, our network
does not look at the text itself,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:23.430
but rather at a vector representation of this
text.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:28.510
These vectors are pre-computed with a word
embedding method of our choice.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:32.010
And these vectors, go into the self-attention
layer.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:36.761
Self-attention is very similar to attention,
which we remember computes an importance score

00:03:36.761 --> 00:03:38.630
for each word vector.

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:44.610
So in attention, if we want to translate this
sentence, attention computes importance between

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:50.740
two different sequences and tells us that
for translating “rabbit” to “Hase”,

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:55.150
we might want to focus to both “rabbit”
and “it”, because they both refer to the

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.520
same entity.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:02.099
This kind of attention we want to use in the
decoder between encoder layers and decoder

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:03.450
layers.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:08.360
Self-attention is good at modelling dependencies
between different parts of the sequence.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.910
For example to understand dependencies between
words in a sentence.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:17.850
These might be syntactic dependencies or coreference,
like in this example here.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:22.770
Self-attention computes importance scores
between words of the same sequence.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:26.590
And the best thing about Transformers is the
multi-head attention.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:32.060
Where the attention we know, is not computed
once, as there is no single attention pattern

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:37.120
that helps in some cases, but there could
be multiple attentions computed at the same

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.300
time, called attention heads.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:46.400
In this way, instead of one pattern, we have
7, 9, or 12 or as many attention heads as

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.620
we want and these are regarded at the same
time!

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.740
Now back to our figure here.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:56.490
After the self-attention layer computed on
the input its output vectors, taking into

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:02.180
account the dependencies of words between
each other, it passes its vectors on to simple,

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:08.730
feed forward neural layers; these sending
the processed output further to the next encoder

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:12.750
layer formed after the same encoder blueprint.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:18.340
We have to stop here shortly and observe how
each word flows on it’s own path and all

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:23.370
these computations per path can be done in
parallel, in contrast to recurrent neural

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:27.699
networks, where one word has to wait for the
output from the previous word.

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:33.520
This parallelization speeds up training considerably,
this is why Transformers can be trained relatively

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:36.730
quickly on huge amounts of text.

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:41.960
But if everything here has it’s own path
looking at the neighbors only through self-attention,

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:44.520
there is yet no way to model word order!

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.280
What to do now??

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.820
Well, researchers have a very simple solution
for this problem.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:55.320
They take every input vector and add a positional
encoding vector, meaning that they add to

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:01.680
each word representation a so-to-say unique
ID, corresponding to the position in the sentence!

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:02.680
Done!

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:07.470
This is how the network knows the word order
and it knows if it is at the beginning, middle

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.970
or at the end of the sentence.

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:11.120
That was easy…!

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:16.800
One more thing left to mention here: The output
of each layer, here the input,

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.200
is added to the output of the next sublayer.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:25.430
This is called a residual connection and enables
better flow of information through the network,

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:30.060
so that information is not lost by one layer
without a chance for the next layer to recover

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.060
it.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:34.750
And what was shown here for the first layer,
happens in every layer.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:39.620
Now we have the stage set for explaining how
this monster architecture delivers an output:

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:45.020
Because here is where the magic in the decoder
happens: The decoder has the same structure

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:51.230
as the encoder with the only difference, that
it has an encoder-decoder attention layer

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:52.830
in the middle.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:57.970
So the decoder, processes the vectors like
the encoder, but also computes attention between

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:01.880
the decoder vectors and the output of the
first encoder.

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:07.030
This helps the decoder to focus on the appropriate
input parts.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:11.139
And now for the final output computation,
the very last layer of the network is the

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:16.940
softmax layer, which assigns for each word
in the vocabulary a probability and all these

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:18.919
probabilities sum up to 1.

00:07:18.919 --> 00:07:24.060
For visualization purposes, we listed only
3 words of a vocabulary that for English would

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.139
consist of tens of thousands of words.

00:07:27.139 --> 00:07:31.790
The biggest probability here would be ideally
“ich”, as it translates to German the

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:33.370
English “I”.

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:38.250
The process repeats itself for the next position
of the word, which is the second word and

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:43.949
so on, until the model predicts with highest
probability the stop word, where the sentence

00:07:43.949 --> 00:07:45.910
reached its end.

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:49.640
This is what a trained model should be able
to give us.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.750
Reasonable probabilities over this vocabulary.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:58.310
At training time we have first a model unable
to predict reasonable probabilities, so we

00:07:58.310 --> 00:07:59.310
teach it.

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:05.150
We run it on text, these could be sentences
and their translation, where we know what

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:06.850
the next word should be.

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:12.300
And interestingly, we do not predict the next
word necessarily, because we can also train

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:17.610
to predict a randomly masked word and try
to predict it with the softmax.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:20.100
This is called masked word prediction.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.830
If the model did not perform well, since it
“thought”...

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:28.550
Do neural networks think? ...that “Katze”,
meaning cat was the most probable word in

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:33.729
this case, since we
know that the correct word should be “bin”,

00:08:33.729 --> 00:08:38.320
we penalize the model and update it through
backpropagation, until it does reasonably

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.269
well in training.

00:08:40.269 --> 00:08:44.800
And if you are not very much excited yet,
this is the best part about Transformers:

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:50.290
They train very easily with self-supervision,
meaning that they do not necessarily need

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:54.610
annotated training data to be able to capture
human language.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:59.259
Because Transformers are trained to predict
randomly masked words, if we want to use them

00:08:59.259 --> 00:09:05.009
for text completion or if we want the Transformers
to write novels, all they need is text lying

00:09:05.009 --> 00:09:08.550
around on Wikipedia and they can already start
training!

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:14.800
Or even better, we can pre-train them to predict
masked words on large corpora containing news

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:20.580
articles and we can train them further (called
fine-tuning) on less data for other tasks

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:25.550
like sentiment classification, because only
by pre-training to predict words on the news

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:30.399
articles, the Transformer learned something
about sentiment classification already!

00:09:30.399 --> 00:09:35.269
How to test if a model trained on task A,
has also implicitly learned how to model task

00:09:35.269 --> 00:09:38.939
B, we have already explained in the video
linked right now!

00:09:38.939 --> 00:09:53.449
Thanks for watching, do not forget to hit
the like and subscribe buttons, and see you

00:09:53.449 --> 00:10:03.939
next time!

